From 8b25bb5412752f44f0121a333db54558328cb1be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: faliye <faliye@example.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 10:23:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: nhi: re-kick TX ring doorbell on missed producer
 update

Symptom:
Over a thunderbolt-net (tbnet) link between two hosts, a small but
persistent fraction of request/response round trips (0.5~1.7% in
testing, higher under additional driver load) stall for exactly the
TCP minimum RTO (~200ms) before completing, even though the frame in
question reached the peer's NIC with a correct checksum and the peer
had already ACKed it. `nstat` on both ends confirms every stall is a
spurious retransmission (matching TCPDSACKRecv counts): no packet is
actually lost.

Root cause:
When `tb_ring_tx()`/`ring_write_descriptors()` posts a TX descriptor it
signals the controller by writing the new producer index to the ring's
doorbell register (`ring_iowrite_prod()`). On the ASMedia ASM4242 USB4
host router, this doorbell write is occasionally not acted upon: the
descriptor sits fully posted (RING_DESC_POSTED, valid producer index)
but the controller's internal consumer pointer never advances to claim
it, so `tbnet_tx_callback()` is never invoked for that frame.

The frame is not lost -- it is released as soon as *any* later
doorbell write with a different producer value occurs. For interactive
request/response traffic there may be nothing else to send until the
peer's stack, having received no reply, retransmits after its RTO. That
retransmission triggers the peer to send a fresh ACK/DSACK, driving a
new local descriptor and therefore a new (different-valued) doorbell
write, which is exactly what frees the originally stuck frame. Bulk
transfers rarely hit this because the ring stays busy enough that the
next real doorbell write arrives well under 200ms; it becomes visible
almost exclusively on latency-sensitive request/response patterns
(observed while running llama.cpp's RPC backend across two hosts
joined by Thunderbolt).

Confirmed with a kprobe on `tb_ring_tx()`/`tbnet_tx_callback()`
measuring time-in-ring per frame: stalled frames sit for 200-300ms
(matching the request-level stalls almost 1:1), against a normal
steady-state residency under 1ms.

Also confirmed the doorbell is edge-triggered on *value change*, not
level-sensitive: a timer that re-wrote the *same* producer index every
500us while a frame was stuck fired several hundred times across a
single stall with no effect. Only a write that changes the register
value unwedges the ring.

Fix:
Add a per-TX-ring hrtimer, armed whenever `ring_write_descriptors()`
leaves descriptors posted, that polls the ring's hardware consumer
index (read-only half of the same doorbell register) every 500us. If
the consumer index is observed frozen for two consecutive polls while
a producer update is still outstanding, the timer briefly rewrites the
producer register to the parked consumer value and immediately back to
the real producer value. Both writes only ever describe descriptors
the hardware has already been told about, so unlike a naive "kick"
this cannot expose descriptors the hardware doesn't yet own -- an
earlier version of this fix that stepped the producer back by one
slot unconditionally did exactly that on an idle-then-resumed ring and
tripped an IOMMU page fault (AMD-Vi IO_PAGE_FAULT) when the hardware
walked past the actually-posted descriptors. The frozen-consumer check
avoids ever touching a ring that is still making forward progress.

The per-ring timer state is kept in a small static side table rather
than as a new field on `struct tb_ring` / `struct tb_nhi`, so this
does not change either struct's layout or the module's exported symbol
CRCs.

The workaround is unconditional (not gated behind a controller quirk)
since it is a no-op whenever the hardware behaves correctly: the
"frozen consumer for 2 polls" condition should never trigger on a
controller that reliably actioned the original doorbell write. This
was only observed on ASMedia ASM4242 (USB4/TB4, PCI id 1b21:2425); it
has not been characterized on Intel Thunderbolt/USB4 controllers.
Gating it behind a vendor quirk (as nhi_check_quirks() already does
for QUIRK_AUTO_CLEAR_INT/QUIRK_E2E) would be a reasonable follow-up
once it's confirmed Intel silicon doesn't need it, to avoid adding a
timer to controllers that never benefit from it.

Testing:
Reproduced and fixed on two hosts (AMD Ryzen 9 9950X, ASM4242 each)
joined point-to-point over Thunderbolt 5 cabling, running a 16KB
request/response workload over TCP on thunderbolt-net.

Before fix (stock v6.17 nhi/tbnet, all other mitigations disabled --
no TCP_QUICKACK, default rto_min, no application-level keepalive):
0.5-1.7% of round trips stalled for ~203-471ms across five runs of
2000-3000 requests each.

After fix, same conditions, five more runs of 2000-3000 requests each
on each host individually and both together: 0 stalls out of 15000
requests. A kprobe on tbnet_tx_callback() confirms 0 frames observed
resident >50ms post-fix, versus 7-10 per run pre-fix.

Signed-off-by: faliye <faliye@example.com>
Cc: Andreas Noever <andreas.noever@gmail.com>
Cc: Mika Westerberg <westeri@kernel.org>
Cc: Yehezkel Bernat <YehezkelShB@gmail.com>
Cc: linux-usb@vger.kernel.org
Cc: linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/nhi.c | 126 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 126 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/nhi.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/nhi.c
index f3a2264..7679a49 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/nhi.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/nhi.c
@@ -231,6 +231,125 @@ static bool ring_empty(struct tb_ring *ring)
 	return ring->head == ring->tail;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Some controllers (observed on ASMedia ASM4242) occasionally miss the
+ * producer index update for the last posted TX descriptor. The frame then
+ * sits in the ring until the next enqueue rings the doorbell again -- for
+ * request/response traffic that next enqueue is the peer's 200ms RTO
+ * retransmission. Work around it with a per-ring timer that re-writes the
+ * producer index while TX descriptors are still in flight. Kept outside
+ * struct tb_ring so the public ABI (and module CRCs) stay unchanged.
+ */
+#define RING_KICK_INTERVAL_US	500
+#define RING_KICK_MAX		64
+
+static struct ring_kick {
+	struct hrtimer timer;
+	struct tb_ring *ring;		/* NULL = slot free */
+	u16 last_cons;
+	bool stuck;
+} ring_kicks[RING_KICK_MAX];
+static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(ring_kick_lock);
+
+static struct ring_kick *ring_kick_find(struct tb_ring *ring)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < RING_KICK_MAX; i++) {
+		if (READ_ONCE(ring_kicks[i].ring) == ring)
+			return &ring_kicks[i];
+	}
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+static enum hrtimer_restart ring_kick_timerfn(struct hrtimer *t)
+{
+	struct ring_kick *kick = container_of(t, struct ring_kick, timer);
+	struct tb_ring *ring = kick->ring;
+	enum hrtimer_restart ret = HRTIMER_NORESTART;
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	if (!ring)
+		return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&ring->lock, flags);
+	if (ring->running && !ring_empty(ring)) {
+		u32 reg = ioread32(ring_desc_base(ring) + 8);
+		u16 cons = reg & 0xffff;
+		u16 prod = (reg >> 16) & 0xffff;
+		bool pending = prod == ring->head && cons != prod;
+
+		if (pending && kick->stuck && kick->last_cons == cons) {
+			/*
+			 * The controller has sat on posted descriptors with
+			 * a frozen consumer index for two consecutive
+			 * intervals: the missed-doorbell stall. Re-writing
+			 * the same producer value is a no-op (it only reacts
+			 * to a value change), so briefly step the producer
+			 * back to the parked consumer index and restore it.
+			 * Both writes expose only descriptors the hardware
+			 * already owns. The frozen-consumer guard keeps this
+			 * away from a live ring, where a transient backward
+			 * producer would expose the whole ring of unposted
+			 * descriptors (observed as AMD-Vi IO_PAGE_FAULT).
+			 */
+			ring_iowrite_prod(ring, cons);
+			/* two distinct change events, not one coalesced */
+			ioread32(ring_desc_base(ring) + 8);
+			ring_iowrite_prod(ring, ring->head);
+			kick->stuck = false;
+		} else {
+			kick->stuck = pending;
+			kick->last_cons = cons;
+		}
+		hrtimer_forward_now(t, us_to_ktime(RING_KICK_INTERVAL_US));
+		ret = HRTIMER_RESTART;
+	} else {
+		kick->stuck = false;
+	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ring->lock, flags);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/* Called without ring->lock (ring not yet published) */
+static void ring_kick_register(struct tb_ring *ring)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	spin_lock(&ring_kick_lock);
+	for (i = 0; i < RING_KICK_MAX; i++) {
+		if (!ring_kicks[i].ring) {
+			hrtimer_setup(&ring_kicks[i].timer, ring_kick_timerfn,
+				      CLOCK_MONOTONIC, HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
+			WRITE_ONCE(ring_kicks[i].ring, ring);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	spin_unlock(&ring_kick_lock);
+}
+
+/* Must be called without ring->lock held */
+static void ring_kick_unregister(struct tb_ring *ring, bool release)
+{
+	struct ring_kick *kick = ring_kick_find(ring);
+
+	if (!kick)
+		return;
+	hrtimer_cancel(&kick->timer);
+	if (release)
+		WRITE_ONCE(kick->ring, NULL);
+}
+
+/* ring->lock is held */
+static void ring_kick_arm(struct tb_ring *ring)
+{
+	struct ring_kick *kick = ring_kick_find(ring);
+
+	if (kick)
+		hrtimer_start(&kick->timer, us_to_ktime(RING_KICK_INTERVAL_US),
+			      HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
+}
+
 /*
  * ring_write_descriptors() - post frames from ring->queue to the controller
  *
@@ -259,6 +378,9 @@ static void ring_write_descriptors(struct tb_ring *ring)
 		else
 			ring_iowrite_cons(ring, ring->head);
 	}
+
+	if (ring->is_tx && !ring_empty(ring))
+		ring_kick_arm(ring);
 }
 
 /*
@@ -613,6 +735,8 @@ static struct tb_ring *tb_ring_alloc(struct tb_nhi *nhi, u32 hop, int size,
 	if (!ring->descriptors)
 		goto err_free_ring;
 
+	ring_kick_register(ring);
+
 	if (ring_request_msix(ring, flags & RING_FLAG_NO_SUSPEND))
 		goto err_free_descs;
 
@@ -763,6 +887,7 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(tb_ring_start);
  */
 void tb_ring_stop(struct tb_ring *ring)
 {
+	ring_kick_unregister(ring, false);
 	spin_lock_irq(&ring->nhi->lock);
 	spin_lock(&ring->lock);
 	dev_dbg(&ring->nhi->pdev->dev, "stopping %s %d\n",
@@ -808,6 +933,7 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(tb_ring_stop);
  */
 void tb_ring_free(struct tb_ring *ring)
 {
+	ring_kick_unregister(ring, true);
 	spin_lock_irq(&ring->nhi->lock);
 	/*
 	 * Dissociate the ring from the NHI. This also ensures that
-- 
